{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that preferring corroboration is a 'tautological selection' fails because it conflates 'informational content' with 'empirical survival.' Popper explicitly states that higher content makes a theory *more* falsifiable (easier to kill), not easier to survive. Therefore, selecting the 'best-corroborated' theory is not tautologically selecting the 'highest content' one (as high-content theories often fail tests); it is selecting the one that has surprisingly survived despite its high risk. This selection of the 'survivor' over the 'non-survivor' or 'lower-content survivor' implicitly relies on the inductive assumption that past survival is a marker of future reliability, which is exactly the induction Popper sought to reject.","premise":"Popper defines corroboration as a report on how severely a theory has been tested and how well it has stood up to those tests, distinguishing it from probability or content alone.","premises_essential":["82c3669d1818dd8fb43a8eed8ca120c9ed9bf83c7455adbac74bc07cc3b9f43e"],"successor_question":"If corroboration is not an inductive guide to truth or future success, what rational basis other than psychological habit distinguishes choosing a highly corroborated theory over a refuted one for practical action?","target":"82c3669d1818dd8fb43a8eed8ca120c9ed9bf83c7455adbac74bc07cc3b9f43e"}]}